  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Plurality (first-past-the-post)', note: 'Most votes wins. Simple, decisive — but votes for losers "waste", so similar parties split support (the "spoiler" problem), and two-party systems emerge (Duverger\'s law).' },
    { math: '2 · Proportional representation', note: 'Seats match vote shares. Fair to small parties, coalition governments normal — but fragmentation and king-maker small parties come with it.' },
    { math: '3 · Ranked choice (IRV) and runoffs', note: 'Rank candidates; losers eliminated, votes transfer until someone passes 50%. Kills spoilers — but can produce counterintuitive winners and still isn\'t "fair" by every criterion (no system is).' },
    { math: '4 · Arrow\'s impossibility theorem (1951)', note: 'No rank-order voting system with 3+ options can satisfy a handful of reasonable fairness conditions simultaneously. Not a cynical joke — a mathematical result. Electoral design is choosing WHICH unfairness you can live with.' },
