<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/voting-systems-and-electoral-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Electoral systems are <strong>translation rules between votes and power</strong> — and the
    rule chosen shapes the party system, the campaigns and the outcomes. The mathematics is
    genuinely surprising: fairness criteria provably conflict (Arrow), so every democracy is
    running a chosen compromise, not the correct answer.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the main systems and the theorem that haunts them all" widget="equationStep" config={voteCfg} />

  <h2>Design choices beyond the ballot</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Districts vs at-large:</strong> single-member districts (local representation, majority bias) vs national lists (proportionality, party control of candidates). The trade-off is representation-of-place vs representation-of-proportion.</li>
    <li><strong>Gerrymandering:</strong> drawing district lines to manufacture outcomes. Detection is now statistical (efficiency gap, ensemble methods — the statistics ladder's simulations of fair maps). Courts are digesting the maths.</li>
    <li><strong>Compulsory voting and turnout:</strong> Australia's compulsory turnout (~90%) vs voluntary (~50–65% elsewhere). Who turns out — not just who prefers what — decides elections; every turnout rule is a distributional choice.</li>
    <li><strong>Referendums:</strong> direct democracy for big questions (Brexit) — binary framing of complex issues, vulnerable to the framing lesson's effects. The design question: which decisions belong to voters directly, and which to representatives deliberating?</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Arrow, Social Choice and Individual Values (1951); Duverger (1954); Reynolds, Reilly & Ellis, Electoral System Design (International IDEA).</p>
</TopicPage>
